 computer readable program code that when executed causes the computer to route, at the termination of a wait period during which the different address is not received, the envelope back to the mail item digitization process; 
 computer readable prom code that when executed causes the computer to route an electronic image of a part of the mail item to a destination corresponding to the part; 
 computer readable program code that when executed causes the computer to apply at electronic classification network to an input vector that includes:
 a physical parameter vector based on output from an optical probe of the envelope; and 
 a vector that includes a probability of a match between a text string vector from the mail item part and a library corresponding to the destination; and 

 computer readable program code that when executed causes the computer to receive from the network an output vector indicating a match with the destination. 



       6. The article of  claim 5  further comprising:
 computer readable program code that when executed causes the computer to receive the different address; 
 computer readable program code that when executed causes a labeler to apply to the envelope a mailing label including the different address; and 
 computer readable program code that when executed causes the computer to routing the envelope from the holding bin to a mail delivery service. 



       7. The article of  claim 5  further comprising:
 computer readable program code that when executed causes the computer to apply an electronic classification network to an input vector that includes:
 a physical parameter vector based on output from an optical probe of the envelope; 
 a string vector that includes a text string vector from the first mail item part; and 

 computer readable program code that when executed causes the computer to receive from the network an output vector indicating a match with the destination. 



       8. The method of  claim 5  wherein the routing comprises flagging the electronic image as exceptional based on the printed message. 


       9. One or more non-transitory computer-readable media storing computer-executable instructions which, when executed by one or more processors of a computer system, perform a method for applying a mailing label, the method comprising:
 registering, by the one or more processors, a mail item received from a mail delivery service, the mail item including on an exterior surface of an envelope a printed message indicating that a customer name appearing on the mail item does not correspond to an address on the mail item; 
 capturing, by an imaging device, a delivery error segment of the surface; 
